At input.rs:1, &String unnecessarily requires a particular owning container even though the function only reads string contents. Under API-01, use:

pub fn byte_len(text: &str) -> usize {
    text.len()
}

This accepts string slices directly and lets String owners borrow their existing storage with byte_len(&owned_string), without allocating, copying, or transferring ownership. Existing calls that pass &String remain valid through deref coercion. Taking an owned String or using Into<String> would impose unnecessary ownership/allocation concerns for this operation. Static review only; no build or tests run.

At input.rs:5, unwrap turns an expected missing/unreadable configuration file into a panic. ERR-01 requires recoverable I/O errors to be returned with their cause. Change the allowed signature and propagate the original error:

pub fn load_config(path: &Path) -> std::io::Result<String> {
    fs::read_to_string(path)
}

The caller should handle the Err and show a useful user-facing error, including the path if appropriate. Replacing unwrap with expect would still panic and would not fix the error contract. Static review only; no build or tests run.

Keep rust-toolchain.toml pinned to 1.94.1 and keep rustfmt.toml's max_width = 90. From the actual Cargo project root, use its existing formatting entrypoint or cargo +1.94.1 fmt --check. The ordinary multiline form of input.rs would be:

pub fn doubled(value: u32) -> u32 {
